# Tutorial: Bug Bounty Hunting for Prototype Pollution

## What it is
Prototype pollution is a type of vulnerability that occurs in JavaScript when properties of Object.prototype are modified. This is particularly risky because JavaScript objects are dynamic and we can add properties to them at any time. Almost all objects in JavaScript inherit from Object.prototype, making it a potential attack vector [1].

## Why it happens
The underlying flaw is that the product receives input from an upstream component that specifies attributes to initialize or update in an object, but it does not properly control modifications of attributes of the object prototype [2]. This means that an attacker can inject attributes that are used in other components by adding or modifying attributes of an object prototype. This can be problematic if the product depends on the existence or non-existence of certain attributes, or uses predefined attributes of the object prototype [2].

## How to find it
Prototype pollution is reachable through any input whose keys end up as object property names. Review the application for inputs that could be manipulated by users, such as URL query strings, URL fragments (hash), JSON inputs, and form data [3].

## A worked example
Consider a naive recursive merge function that copies every key of an attacker-controlled object into a target:

```javascript
function merge(target, source) {
    for (const key in source) {
        if (typeof source[key] === "object" && typeof target[key] === "object") {
            merge(target[key], source[key]);
        } else {
            target[key] = source[key];
        }
    }
    return target;
}
```

If the source is parsed from user input such as `{"__proto__": {"polluted": "yes"}}`, the assignment walks into `__proto__` and writes onto `Object.prototype`. Afterwards, every object in the runtime inherits the planted property:

```javascript
merge({}, JSON.parse('{"__proto__": {"polluted": "yes"}}'));
({}).polluted;   // "yes"  -> Object.prototype was polluted
```

This example shows how an attacker can exploit a vulnerable merge function to pollute the prototype of objects [3].

## How to test it safely
To test for prototype pollution safely, ensure you are working within a controlled environment such as a lab or on a target explicitly in scope for a program you are authorized to test. Use tools and methods that simulate user inputs to check if the application modifies Object.prototype improperly.

## Key takeaways
- Prototype pollution occurs when JavaScript objects inherit unintended properties from Object.prototype [1].
- Attackers can exploit this by injecting attributes into Object.prototype through user-controllable inputs [2].
- Review applications for inputs that could manipulate object properties, such as URL query strings and JSON inputs [3].

